]]\n[\"XC.EXT.plugins\", \"Plugin architecture & discovery\", \"plugin-system\", \"E\"]\n[\"XC.EXT.plugin-deps\", \"Plugin packaging & dependency resolution\", \"plugin-system\", \"E\"]\n[\"XC.EXT.mod-sdk\", \"Mod SDK & tooling\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\"]\n[\"XC.EXT.mod-loading\", \"Mod loading & override precedence\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"package-formats-vfs\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.ugc\", \"UGC pipeline\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"platform-services\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.mod-compat\", \"Mod compatibility across patches\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"api-lifecycle-migration\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.plugin-trust\", \"Plugin signature & permission manifest fields\", \"plugin-system\", \"E\"]\n[\"XC.EXT.player-creation\", \"Player-facing in-game creation mode (runtime editing subset, gamepad/touch placement, budgets, undo, publish/versioning) in client targets\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"world-editor-viewport\", \"editor-architect\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.ugc-discovery\", \"UGC browse/rate/play boundary with moderation & entitlement hooks\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"online-services-liveops\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.mod-editor\", \"Redistributable modder editor/SDK build (licence/NDA stripping, sandboxed tool plugins)\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"packaging-release-patching\", \"platform-architect\", \"security-engineering\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.fork-integration\", \"Customization-seam catalogue, licensee modification manifest, upstream-merge tooling & reports\", \"api-lifecycle-migration\", \"E\", [\"build-release-architect\", \"collaboration-version-control\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.ugc-integrity\", \"Mod/UGC distribution integrity: package signing via C-SIGN, author identity & 2FA at the distribution boundary, update pinning/review, remote revocation/kill switch, native-code mod policy (sandboxed by default, native only by explicit player opt-in)\", \"modding-ugc\", \"E\", [\"security-engineering\", \"online-services-liveops\", \"platform-services\"]]\n[\"XC.EXT.generated-assets\", \"Runtime validation & cooking of player-prompted generated assets under XC.SEC.genai\", \"modding-ugc\", \"M\", [\"asset-cook-processors\", \"security-engineering\", \"online-services-liveops\"]]\n['ugc'] ['C-VFS', 'C-SCRIPT', 'C-PLUGIN', 'C-SVC', 'C-SIGN'] ['C-EDCMD', 'C-EDHOST', 'C-COOK']\ngameplay-systems-toolkit consumes ['C-EDIT?']\n{\n \"id\": \"C-GRAPH\",\n \"layer\": 5,\n \"owner\": \"graph-editor-framework\",\n \"name\": \"Graph authoring & compilation\",\n \"summary\": \"Node/pin model, validation, compilation hooks for domain graphs.\",\n \"requires\": [\n  \"C-EDCMD\"\n ],\n \"conformance\": true,\n \"oracle_author\": \"material-system\"\n}","is_error":false}]},"uuid":"c876b25d-d8cf-49ce-af45-bd127b38c4d0","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:59:28.763Z","sourceToolAssistantUUID":"57739322-25f6-4168-a64e-debb24ba56b0","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"c876b25d-d8cf-49ce-af45-bd127b38c4d0","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8d66f27603101d9","attachment":{"type":"total_tokens_reminder","text":"<total_tokens>14850645 tokens left</total_tokens>"},"type":"attachment","uuid":"1d9126bb-1fca-4ed2-b0d3-d14cc56d5330","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:59:28.766Z","rendered":[{"content":"<system-reminder>\n<total_tokens>14850645 tokens left</total_tokens>\n</system-reminder>"}],"userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"1d9126bb-1fca-4ed2-b0d3-d14cc56d5330","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8d66f27603101d9","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfY61KcGJmSdXcAnPscJz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3058,"cache_read_input_tokens":148181,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3058,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CfY61KHfW1vovXcsbR84p","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"d72d7dc3-6caf-4ec0-9c72-6dc5fd65b60c","timestamp":"2026-09-29T16:59:52.379Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
{"parentUuid":"d72d7dc3-6caf-4ec0-9c72-6dc5fd65b60c","isSidechain":true,"agentId":"ac8d66f27603101d9","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfY61KcGJmSdXcAnPscJz","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_0114dDAfj9TzAqMXYnX7YhfV","name":"Write","input":{"file_path":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-2/adjudication.md","content":"# Round 2 — independent adjudication of partial dispositions\n\nAdjudicator scope: the 24 findings in `for-adjudication.md`. Every claimed change was checked against the current `data/*.json`, `docs/00`, `docs/06` and `scripts/check.py`. `python3 scripts/check.py` reports 0 errors, 0 warnings. Builder change logs and `r2_*.py` were not read.\n\n---\n\n### K-ARCH-14 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `network-architect` NR \"Matchmaking services\" → `online-services-liveops` (owner of `PLAT.SVC.matchmaking`). `dedicated-server` NR \"Fleet operation\" → `external:backend`; `NET.SRV.orchestration` contributor is now `online-services-liveops` (`platform-services` removed). `network-transport` NR \"Relay service operation\" → `external:backend`. `water-ocean` NR \"Buoyancy dynamics\" → `rigid-body-dynamics`. `WLD.ENV.buoyancy` is renamed to \"Water query service (C-ENV provider…)\" and its contributors are `rigid-body-dynamics` and `vehicle-physics` (`character-physics` removed). `PHY.FLUID.shallow` is gone and its content now sits in `WLD.ENV.water-interaction`. `crowd-simulation` purpose now says \"individual-agent local avoidance consumed from C-NAV\". `navigation-pathfinding` NR says \"Flow-field & mass-agent avoidance → crowd-simulation\", and it keeps `GAM.AI.local-avoidance`. `spatial-audio-acoustics` purpose no longer mentions panning or distance. `build-release-architect` purpose says \"pipeline architecture (execution by ci-cd-automation)\" and adds the NR \"Pipeline orchestration → ci-cd-automation\".\n- Ruling rationale: all seven routes the critic named are corrected. The cap-id rename was done as a name change, which has the same effect. A keyword-overlap lint on free NR text would be noisy and non-deterministic, so rejecting it is defensible.\n- Note (non-binding): the disposition's claim that \"authoring, legacy-stance and oracle rules cover the structural part\" is inaccurate. `check.py` (lines 123–131) still checks only that an NR target exists and is not self. A deterministic alternative would let an NR optionally cite a capability id and require that the target owns, contributes to or leads that capability. Consider it for round 3.\n\n### K-ARCH-17 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `plugin-system.parent = core-runtime-architect` (workstream foundation). `modding-ugc.parent = gameplay-architect` (workstream gameplay). `anti-cheat-integrity.parent = security-engineering`, workstream `online`. It owns `XC.SEC.score-integrity`, `XC.SEC.attestation`, `XC.SEC.usermode-anticheat` and `XC.SEC.info-hiding`, and provides `C-INTEGRITY` (L3), which `prediction-rollback`, `dedicated-server`, `character-movement` and others consume.\n- Ruling rationale: the two reparentings are done. Keeping anti-cheat under security-engineering is technically sound. Much of its territory (offline score integrity, device attestation, user-mode posture) is not netcode. Its workstream stays `online`, and 00 §2 delegates arbitration by workstream, so its disputes with replication are settled inside the online workstream, not by the threat-model skill. Its runtime coupling to networking is carried by `C-INTEGRITY`.\n\n### K-COMPLETE-4 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `GAM.AI.team-visibility` (\"Per-faction visibility fields & line-of-sight/FOV (grid/hex/navmesh, GPU variant), explored state, deterministic, feeding fog rendering, AI knowledge and replication relevancy\"), owner `ai-behavior-perception` (profiles `all`), contributors `replication`, `anti-cheat-integrity`, `render-2d-vector`, `crowd-simulation`, maturity E. `crowd-simulation` has profiles `[\"openworld\",\"massim\"]`.\n- Ruling rationale: the omission (no owner for team visibility / FOV) is closed. The owner the builder chose is better than the proposed one: `crowd-simulation` does not exist in min2d roguelike configurations, while `ai-behavior-perception` exists in every profile.\n- Note: the contract path from this capability to replication relevancy and the fog overlay is not wired. This is ruled on under K-GAMEPLAY-12, where the critic asked for it explicitly.\n\n### K-FUTURE-1 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `RND.GRAPH.work-graphs` is X/`experimental`. The new `RND.RHI.work-graph-programs` is X/`experimental` (\"fallback: indirect dispatch\") and has its own radar entry, class X. `RND.GEO.mesh-nodes` also has an X radar entry. `RND.RHI.gpu-work` is E and now covers \"indirect, device-generated commands, backing memory\". `check.py` lines 452–459 enforce radar class = capability maturity, and its selftest mutates the work-graph entry to prove the rule. docs/00 §7 and docs/06 A6 agree.\n- Ruling rationale: the material problem was experimental work graphs presented as a production baseline. That is resolved. Keeping device-generated commands in E is sound: D3D12 ExecuteIndirect with state-changing command signatures and Metal indirect command buffers have shipped for years, and `VK_EXT_device_generated_commands` is a cross-vendor EXT. A separate M row would not change what phase-2 agents freeze.\n\n### K-FUTURE-7 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `ml-inference-runtime` has profiles `[\"all\"]`, parent `core-runtime-architect`, workstream `foundation`, and the new NR \"GPU queue/budget arbitration of C-MLGPU work → gpu-platform-architect\". `C-MLGPU` stays gated. `C-ML` (L2) requires only `C-TASK` and `C-MEM`.\n- Ruling rationale: every structural part of the proposal is applied. Because the profile is `all`, the runtime is in the `minimal-client` closure (75 build skills, closed by `check.py`), which is the proof the proposed `narrative-llm-client` configuration would have given. On-device LLM sequence execution (`ML.RT.sequence-models`) is X and so opt-in through `experimental`. That follows the maturity rules and is not a scale-down defect.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-2 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-ABILITY` L4 (gameplay-systems-toolkit: activation, effects, attributes, tag queries, messages, hit results). `C-CROWD` L4 (crowd-simulation: spawn/despawn, batched queries, LOD/tier hooks). `C-SEQ` L4 (cinematics-sequencer: playback, binding, event/track callbacks, skip/blend). Consumers: `ai-behavior-perception` → `C-ABILITY?`; `gameplay-architect` → `C-SEQ?`, `C-CROWD?`. `C-GAMEDATA` (L3) summary now includes \"gameplay tag registry and tag queries\", and `narrative-dialogue` consumes `C-GAMEDATA?`.\n- Ruling rationale: all three missing surfaces now exist and are consumed. Putting tags in the existing L3 `C-GAMEDATA` meets the critic's own constraint (\"tags must live at L3\"). A separate `C-TAGS` would add nothing. `C-SEQ` at L4 rather than L3 is harmless because its consumers are L4.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-AI` L4 (ai-behavior-perception: brain assignment for BT/state tree/utility/HTN, perception stimulus sources, smart-object claim/release, environment query service; learned/LLM providers plug in through `C-AIAGENT`). Consumers are `gameplay-architect`, `gameplay-systems-toolkit` and `crowd-simulation`. `ai-behavior-perception` consumes `C-MOVE?`. The `C-NAV` summary states \"nav produces path corridors and steering intents and never moves bodies\".\n- Ruling rationale: every item is present. A separate established contract with the gated extension point referenced from it is cleaner than renaming `C-AIAGENT`, because it keeps the gated/optional rule of 00 §7 intact.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-5 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: the `C-CFG` summary now says settings are declared as scoped C-CFG entries (device/user/cloud), and the user layer is a read-only projection populated by the persisted settings store (`GAM.SAVE.settings`). The `C-SAVE` summary adds \"persisted player-settings store per scope, populating the C-CFG user layer\". The `C-A11YRT` summary adds \"accessibility settings are C-CFG settings entries\". `GAM.SAVE.settings` is renamed to \"Persisted player-settings store per scope …, first-boot availability; settings declared through C-CFG\". `C-CFG` is `universal: runtime`, so input-system, accessibility and runtime-scalability can all register settings.\n- Ruling rationale: there is now one declaration path (C-CFG, available to every runtime skill including L1 `runtime-scalability`) and one persistence owner. That is the single-registry outcome the critic asked for, reached without the layering violation that `C-SAVE` consumption from L1 would cause.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-12 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `GAM.AI.team-visibility` exists (see K-COMPLETE-4). `GAM.SYS.markers` exists (gameplay-systems-toolkit, contributors ui-architect, render-architect). `UI.FW.maps` exists (ui-architect). `XC.SEC.info-hiding` exists (anti-cheat-integrity). Missing:\n  - `ai-behavior-perception` does not consume `C-REP`.\n  - The `C-AI` summary does not mention visibility queries.\n  - `anti-cheat-integrity` consumes neither `C-REP` nor `C-AI`. It provides L3 `C-INTEGRITY`, so it cannot consume L4 `C-AI`.\n  - `ui-architect` (L3 `C-UI`) cannot consume the toolkit's L4 contracts, and neither `gameplay-systems-toolkit` nor `C-ABILITY` exposes the marker registry.\n- Ruling rationale: the capabilities and owners are fine, including the owner changes. But the critic explicitly required \"C-REP relevancy hook consumption for the visibility item\". This is the anti-maphack path: the server withholds what a team cannot see. It is absent. As the data stands, the owner of team visibility has no contract through which to feed replication relevancy, fog rendering or the map service. Under 00 §3 its contributors could reach it only through internals. The material problem (a cross-domain system without a working seam) is therefore still half open.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add `C-REP?` to `ai-behavior-perception.consumes`. State in the `C-REP` summary, or in `GAM.AI.team-visibility`, that per-faction visibility registers as a relevancy filter through C-REP relevancy hooks. `XC.SEC.info-hiding` builds on that filter.\n  2. Add \"per-faction visibility / explored-state queries\" to the `C-AI` summary. State that the fog overlay receives the visibility field through a declared `C-FLOW` channel (ai-behavior-perception already consumes C-FLOW).\n  3. Give the marker registry a reachable surface for `UI.FW.maps`. For example, add the marker registry to the `C-ABILITY` (or `C-GAME`) summary and have `gameplay-systems-toolkit` consume `C-UI?` to publish markers into the L3 map service. Alternatively, put a marker-source interface in `C-UI` that the toolkit implements.\n\n### K-LEGACY-7 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `ecs-runtime.tool_consumes = [C-COOK]`, `world-architect.tool_consumes = [C-COOK]`, `render-architect.tool_consumes = [C-EDCMD, C-EDHOST]`, `geometry-pipeline.tool_consumes = [C-COOK]`. `check.py` lines 490–496 fail a runtime owner of a \"tool-side\" capability that has no tool contract. \"Tool-side\" is `TOOL_SIDE` (line 590): `cook step|authoring → runtime baking|bake pipeline|editor render features|generation orchestration`. 00 §3 (\"tool contracts only from tool_consumes\") keeps code that uses `C-COOK`/`C-EDHOST` out of client and server closures.\n- Ruling rationale: the four named capabilities are fixed, and deriving tool-side status from wording instead of a new field would be an acceptable mechanism. However, the regex is tailored to the literal names of the flagged capabilities, not to \"cook/bake/editor territory\" as the disposition claims. A cap such as \"texture baking\", \"cooked X\", \"editor support for Y\" or `XC.EXT.mod-sdk` (\"Mod SDK & tooling\") would not match. The guarantee the critic asked for (any cook/bake/editor capability's owner reaches a tool contract) is therefore not enforced for future edits, and the check's own claim overstates it. A broader wording rule passes against today's data. The only runtime-owned hit without tool contracts under `\\bcook|\\bbak(e|ed|ing)\\b|\\beditor\\b` is `ED.DEBUG.automation-protocol`, a legitimate runtime protocol.\n- Required change: broaden `TOOL_SIDE` in `scripts/check.py` to the general vocabulary, at minimum `\\bcook(ed|ing)?\\b|\\bbak(e|ed|ing)\\b|\\beditor\\b|\\bSDK\\b.*tool|modder editor|authoring tool`. Add an explicit exemption list (initially `ED.DEBUG.automation-protocol`, plus any other runtime protocol the builder justifies). Add a selftest mutation, for example emptying `ecs-runtime.tool_consumes` must go red via `CORE.ECS.baking`, so the guard is known to fire.\n\n### K-LEGACY-12 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `WLD.PART.grid`, `.sources` and `.hlod` carry `[\"lite3d\",\"std3d\"]`. `WLD.PART.server` carries `[\"openworld\"]`, and `WLD.PART.sim-tiers` carries `[\"openworld\",\"massim\"]`. The new `WLD.MODEL.unit-load` (\"Async non-blocking whole-unit load with loading-screen hand-off (minimal/2D default)\") is owned by world-architect, E. L18 stance: \"Async non-blocking loading; cell streaming with budgeted activation for 3D/openworld\", with stance caps `WLD.PART.activation` and `WLD.MODEL.unit-load`.\n- Ruling rationale: minimal and min2d closures no longer contain cell streaming or HLOD, and the new-dogma wording of L18 is fixed. Gating on lite3d/std3d instead of openworld is sound, because linear 3D titles also stream sub-levels.\n- Note (non-binding): `WLD.PART.cook` (\"World → streaming-cell cook step\") is still untagged. Give it the same `[\"lite3d\",\"std3d\"]` tags as `WLD.PART.grid` for consistency.\n\n### K-NET-8 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `NET.ARCH.validation` = \"Netcode oracle scenarios & acceptance thresholds (co-signed by simulation-validation per QA.AGENT.oracle-change-control)\", contributor `simulation-validation`. `QA.SIM.netsim` = \"Deterministic single-process network-simulation runner, metrics & tolerance store\". `QA.FUNC.network` = \"Multi-client bot harness & scripted network sessions\". `NET.TRANS.simulation` = \"Network link conditioner seam (latency, loss, jitter)\". `QA.AGENT.oracle-change-control` requires quality-skill co-signature on every `*.validation` change. The contract conformance suites have independent oracle authors (`C-NET` → online-performance, `C-REP` → prediction-rollback).\n- Ruling rationale: the four-way overlap now has explicit, disjoint boundaries. The self-grading risk is removed by a different mechanism: the network lead can no longer change scenarios or thresholds without the independent quality skill (workstream quality), and the runner and metrics belong to simulation-validation. This matches the physics, animation and audio pattern and is consistent with the render-validation precedent.\n\n### K-NET-19 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `NET.ARCH.meshing` is M, and its radar entry is class M (evidence: Star Citizen live since late 2024; fallback: zoning). docs/06 A5 says \"`emerging` (M) in data and radar\". New entries: `NET.TRANS.l4s` (X, radar, fallback standard CC), `NET.REP.moq-spectator` (X, radar, fallback delayed spectator replication), `XC.SEC.behavioral-detection` (M, radar, fallback rule-based validation), `XC.SEC.usermode-anticheat` (M, radar \"Windows Resiliency Initiative… Proton\", fallback kernel middleware where permitted).\n- Ruling rationale: the three label sources now agree, which was part (a). Every radar gap in part (b) has an owned, fallback-bearing entry. M for meshing is a justified reading of \"emerging production\" given a live commercial deployment.\n\n### K-PLATFORM-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-PAL.needs_implementer = true`. The summary says \"interfaces and policy owned by platform-architect; each platform's implementation is a registered backend implemented by that platform's skill\". `implements` contains `C-PAL` for platform-desktop, -console, -mobile, -web and -server-host. There are new per-platform capabilities `PLAT.DESK.pal`, `PLAT.CON.pal`, `PLAT.MOB.pal`, `PLAT.WEB.pal` and `PLAT.SRV.pal`. `check.py` enforces a per-platform implementer for every `needs_implementer` contract in each configuration. `ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends` lists the slots.\n- Ruling rationale: who writes the Win32 window versus the PAL interface is now decided in data, and it is proven per configuration and platform. A separate `slots` field would duplicate the capability text.\n\n### K-PLATFORM-6 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `platform-console` and `rhi-console` have `access = \"nda:per-platform-holder\"` and `instances = \"one agent per platform-holder family\"`. `check.py` lines 236–239 require console-only skills to be NDA and NDA skills to be console-only. The new `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots` (platform-console) lists IO/decompression, audio endpoints, save storage, crash upload, system keyboard and entitlement SDK. The `C-ORCH` summary adds access classes and NDA routing. Still present: `RES.IO.backends` = \"Async IO backends (io_uring, IOCP, DirectStorage, **console APIs**)\", owned by public `async-io-storage`. `PLAT.XR.runtime-backends` = \"… **console XR SDK (confidential slot)**\", owned by public `xr-runtime` and not listed in `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots`.\n- Ruling rationale: the access class, the per-holder instantiation and the consolidated slot capability are acceptable alternative mechanisms. However, the critic's core issue was confidential slot implementations owned by skills with no access class, which program-orchestration cannot route. Two public skills still own confidential console territory, and one of them overlaps directly with `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots` (IO). The critic's proposed guard (\"no public skill owns a capability marked nda\") has no equivalent, which is why `check.py` does not catch either case.\n- Required change:\n  1. Reword `RES.IO.backends` to drop \"console APIs\", or say \"console variants via PLAT.CON.confidential-slots\".\n  2. Move the console XR SDK slot out of `PLAT.XR.runtime-backends` into `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots`, adding `xr-runtime` as a contributor.\n  3. Add a `check.py` rule, with a selftest mutation: a capability whose name matches `confidential|console (API|SDK)` (exempting the mechanism capability `PLAT.PAL.confidential-extensions` and public-interface wording such as \"behind public interfaces\") must be owned by a skill whose `access` starts with `nda`.\n\n### K-PLATFORM-12 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: targets are platform-mobile `[\"client\",\"tools\"]`, platform-console `[\"client\",\"tools\"]` and platform-web `[\"client\",\"tools\"]`. `C-TARGETPLAT` is an L5 `needs_implementer` contract (\"per-target-platform tool modules: cook variants, shader backend hook, packager, deploy/launch/debug\"), implemented by all platform skills and tool-consumed by content-pipeline-architect and packaging-release-patching.\n- Ruling rationale: the spurious headless-client/server targets are removed. The tools target now has real content (target-platform tool modules loaded by the PC editor and cooker) and does not imply web- or console-hosted tools.\n\n### K-PROD-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `reference-games` has kind `runtime`, targets client/headless-client/server/tools, and consumes `C-GAME, C-INPUT?, C-UI?, C-SCRIPT?, C-GAMEDATA?, C-API`. It owns new `QA.REF.game-code` (public-API only), `QA.REF.content-acquisition` (licensed/purchased/scanned/procedural/generated with rights manifest; contributors content-pipeline-architect, which owns `CNT.ID.rights`, and procedural-generation) and `QA.REF.upkeep` (migration each release, feeding `ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus`). It is in milestone M1, and `QA.REF.ladder` is a gate.\n- Ruling rationale: the three unowned responsibilities now have an owner that is a real build skill consuming the public API, which is the dogfooding the critic wanted. Whether that owner is staffed as one agent or a team is a staffing matter that `ARCH.ORG.staffing` governs, not a boundary defect.\n- Note (non-binding): `ai-assisted-authoring` (proposed contributor) is not on `QA.REF.content-acquisition`. It reaches the rights chain through `CNT.ID.rights`/`ED.AI.provenance`, so this is not material.\n\n### K-PROD-7 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: the milestones run M0–M7. M1 is \"Indie 2D slice on PC\" (runtime only). M2 is \"ships everywhere, with tools\" and includes a TRC/store-review dry run (gate `QA.CERT.prechecks`), with editor contracts drafted at M2. M4 adds \"patch + DLC + staged rollout + rollback executed on the online reference game\" (gates `BLD.REL.staged-rollout`, `BLD.REL.rollback`). M7 is \"Engine 1.0 / first LTS\": corpus upgrade, release notes, backport stream, console cert pass, live-ops and end-of-service rehearsal (gates `ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus`, `XC.EXT.lts`, `BLD.REL.end-of-service`, `PLAT.LIVE.operations`). I computed the M1 closure: 39 of the 44 M1 skills are required by `indie-2d-client@pc ∪ minimal-client@pc` beyond M0. The other 5 (network-architect, network-transport, replication, prediction-rollback, net-session) are placed there deliberately as contract-drafting design authorities, per the M1 exit text.\n- Ruling rationale: every missing ship-lifecycle milestone the critic listed is present, and the M1a/M1b split is in effect: editor/tools and non-PC platforms move to M2. The remaining M1 size is forced by the minimal-client closure, not by bundling, except for five network authorities placed early on purpose.\n- Notes (non-binding): (1) the disposition's statement that the configuration \"requires\" all 44 skills is accurate for 39. (2) The critic's last bullet, milestone exit as test-architect evidence plus a human gate, is only partly covered: the gates are capabilities with owners, and the program-orchestration vs reference-games independence pair exists. `ARCH.ORG.human-gates` does not list milestone exit sign-off. Adding it is recommended.\n\n### K-SEC-14 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `BLD.SYS.dev-surface-exclusion` (\"Dev-surface manifest per skill, fitness function and binary scan proving shipping configurations contain none; allowed shipping surfaces authenticated via C-IPC\"), owned by build-system-toolchains with contributors security-engineering and architecture-governance. `dev_surfaces` is declared on platform-architect (devlink), observability-telemetry (remote diagnostics), visual-debugging-tools (console & cheats, automation endpoint), hot-reload-iteration (live coding, reload endpoint) and scripting-runtime (script debugger (DAP)). `ED.ARCH.remote` belongs to `editor-architect`, which is tool-only, so its on-device endpoint is the devlink/automation/reload surfaces already declared. The M1 gate includes `BLD.SYS.dev-surface-exclusion`.\n- Ruling rationale: this is what the critic proposed, and a fitness function plus binary scan as the capability's deliverable, gated at M1, is the mechanism the critic asked for. The critic did not ask for a check.py rule.\n- Note (non-binding): the `skills.json` `_doc` does not document the `dev_surfaces` field. The `C-IPC` summary still says only \"dev-only port security\" and could name the authenticated shipping-surface rule.\n\n### K-SIM-1 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `CORE.FRAME.pipelining` = \"Frame pipelining & frames in flight (incl. whether any dedicated render/RHI thread exists, by ADR)\". L03's stance is \"Render work as tasks in overlapping task-graph frames; dedicated threads only by ADR\", with `stance_capabilities` `CORE.FRAME.pipelining`, `RND.GPU.recording` and `CORE.JOBS.thread-model`. `check.py` lines 473–488 require every legacy pattern to name existing stance capabilities that its justification owner owns, contributes to or leads.\n- Ruling rationale: the capability no longer prescribes the legacy two-thread model and is tied to the L03 stance. Matching capability names against prose detection strings would be brittle. Linking every legacy pattern to the capabilities that carry its modern stance is a sound substitute, and it would have caught this case because the stance capability's wording is now reviewed against L03.\n\n### K-SIM-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `fluid-simulation.profiles = [\"aaa\",\"sandbox\",\"massim\"]`. `PHY.FLUID.cellular` (\"Grid/cellular material & fluid simulation (falling sand, liquid/gas/heat grids), 2D-capable, deterministic\"), E, contributor `physics-2d`. The configuration `sandbox-2d-client` (`min2d + sandbox`, pc) contains `fluid-simulation` and `PHY.FLUID.cellular` and is closed by `check.py`.\n- Ruling rationale: the critic's proposal explicitly allowed gating on sandbox/massim in addition to aaa. The missing Noita/ONI-class capability exists and a 2D configuration proves it.\n\n### K-SYSTEMS-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-FRAME` is consumed by audio-architect, spatial-transforms, scripting-runtime, navigation-pathfinding, ai-behavior-perception, ui-architect, ml-inference-runtime and prediction-rollback. `C-FLOW` is consumed by audio-architect, physics-architect, animation-architect, replication, ai-behavior-perception, input-system and render-architect.\n- Ruling rationale: every edge the critic proposed exists. The proposed rule turns on \"schedules per-frame work\", which is not decidable from current data. Rejecting it is reasonable, and the concrete undeclared dependencies that caused the problem are gone.\n\n### K-TEST-2 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: the `QA.SIM` capabilities are renamed to artifacts: `stability-suite` (\"runner, metrics (penetration, energy drift) & tolerance store\"), `golden-traces` (\"comparison harness & baseline store\"), `netsim` (\"runner, metrics & tolerance store\"), `animation` (\"error metrics … & tolerance store\") and `audio` (\"metric library (BS.1770 loudness, glitch detection) & offline-render comparison harness\"). The domain `*.ARCH.validation` caps keep the scenario suites under `QA.AGENT.oracle-change-control`. `simulation-validation.consumes = [C-PHYS, C-ANIM, C-AUDIO, C-NET?, C-REPLAY]`. Its NR \"Domain scenario definitions\" now points to an explicit list (physics-, animation-, audio-, network-architect), and the `owning-skill` target is gone. The net four-way boundary is explicit (see K-NET-8).\n- Ruling rationale: every part of the proposal is applied. The retained split (scenarios in the domain, co-signed) is what the critic proposed (\"Keep scene and scenario definitions in the *.ARCH.validation caps\").\n\n### K-TOOLS-8 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `C-EDIT` L4 (modding-ugc): serializable commands with the schema shared with C-EDCMD per `ED.ARCH.transactions` (which lists modding-ugc as contributor), undo, selection, permissions, budgets, publish/version. It is consumed by `gameplay-systems-toolkit` (`C-EDIT?`). New `XC.EXT.player-creation`, `XC.EXT.ugc-discovery` and `XC.EXT.mod-editor` (licence/NDA stripping, sandboxed tool plugins; contributors packaging-release-patching, platform-architect, security-engineering). `modding-ugc.tool_consumes = [C-EDCMD, C-EDHOST, C-COOK]`. Missing: `C-GRAPH` is still L5 tool-only (\"Graph authoring & compilation\"). `RND.SHADER.untrusted` covers only \"GPU-DoS limits for UGC-authored graphs/shaders\", and no capability or contract provides a runtime (client-target) graph compile path. `TOOL_SIDE` does not match `XC.EXT.mod-sdk` (\"Mod SDK & tooling\") or `XC.EXT.mod-editor`.\n- Ruling rationale: runtime edit sessions, the redistributable modder editor and modding-ugc's tool contracts are resolved. Sharing the command schema through `ED.ARCH.transactions` is a sound way to avoid an editor→UGC dependency. Two parts of the finding are still open:\n  - The critic named the missing \"runtime graph-compile path\" for in-client graph authoring (Dreams-class, and the UGC shaders `RND.SHADER.untrusted` anticipates). Nothing owns it, and the only compile surface (`C-GRAPH`) cannot ship in client builds.\n  - The disposition says the `mod-sdk` name rule is \"replaced by the general tool-side rule\", but that rule's regex does not match either mod-SDK capability, so the replacement does not exist in `check.py`.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add a capability for the client-side graph path, for example `RND.SHADER.ugc-runtime-compile` / `XC.EXT.runtime-graphs`: \"Runtime compilation of UGC-authored graphs (restricted node set, validation, shader/VM compile in client targets under RND.SHADER.untrusted limits)\", owner `shader-system` or `modding-ugc`, profile `ugc`, contributor `graph-editor-framework`. Expose it through `C-EDIT` (or a new L3/L4 runtime graph contract), and state that `C-GRAPH`'s node/pin model is the shared schema, as is done for commands.\n  2. Make the tool-side rule cover `XC.EXT.mod-sdk` and `XC.EXT.mod-editor`. This is satisfied by the broadened `TOOL_SIDE` required under K-LEGACY-7 if it includes `\\bSDK\\b.*tool|modder editor`.\n\n---\n\n**Summary: 24 findings adjudicated — 20 UPHOLD, 4 OVERTURN (K-GAMEPLAY-12, K-LEGACY-7, K-PLATFORM-6, K-TOOLS-8).**\n"},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3058,"cache_read_input_tokens":148181,"output_tokens":13959,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":2377},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3058},"inference_geo":"not_available","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":13959,"cache_read_input_tokens":148181,"cache_creation_input_tokens":3058,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":3058,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"wireToolInputs":{"toolu_0114dDAfj9TzAqMXYnX7YhfV":{"file_path":"/home/user/skills/engine-skill-framework/gauntlet/round-2/adjudication.md","content":"# Round 2 — independent adjudication of partial dispositions\n\nAdjudicator scope: the 24 findings in `for-adjudication.md`. Every claimed change was checked against the current `data/*.json`, `docs/00`, `docs/06` and `scripts/check.py`. `python3 scripts/check.py` reports 0 errors, 0 warnings. Builder change logs and `r2_*.py` were not read.\n\n---\n\n### K-ARCH-14 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `network-architect` NR \"Matchmaking services\" → `online-services-liveops` (owner of `PLAT.SVC.matchmaking`). `dedicated-server` NR \"Fleet operation\" → `external:backend`; `NET.SRV.orchestration` contributor is now `online-services-liveops` (`platform-services` removed). `network-transport` NR \"Relay service operation\" → `external:backend`. `water-ocean` NR \"Buoyancy dynamics\" → `rigid-body-dynamics`. `WLD.ENV.buoyancy` is renamed to \"Water query service (C-ENV provider…)\" and its contributors are `rigid-body-dynamics` and `vehicle-physics` (`character-physics` removed). `PHY.FLUID.shallow` is gone and its content now sits in `WLD.ENV.water-interaction`. `crowd-simulation` purpose now says \"individual-agent local avoidance consumed from C-NAV\". `navigation-pathfinding` NR says \"Flow-field & mass-agent avoidance → crowd-simulation\", and it keeps `GAM.AI.local-avoidance`. `spatial-audio-acoustics` purpose no longer mentions panning or distance. `build-release-architect` purpose says \"pipeline architecture (execution by ci-cd-automation)\" and adds the NR \"Pipeline orchestration → ci-cd-automation\".\n- Ruling rationale: all seven routes the critic named are corrected. The cap-id rename was done as a name change, which has the same effect. A keyword-overlap lint on free NR text would be noisy and non-deterministic, so rejecting it is defensible.\n- Note (non-binding): the disposition's claim that \"authoring, legacy-stance and oracle rules cover the structural part\" is inaccurate. `check.py` (lines 123–131) still checks only that an NR target exists and is not self. A deterministic alternative would let an NR optionally cite a capability id and require that the target owns, contributes to or leads that capability. Consider it for round 3.\n\n### K-ARCH-17 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `plugin-system.parent = core-runtime-architect` (workstream foundation). `modding-ugc.parent = gameplay-architect` (workstream gameplay). `anti-cheat-integrity.parent = security-engineering`, workstream `online`. It owns `XC.SEC.score-integrity`, `XC.SEC.attestation`, `XC.SEC.usermode-anticheat` and `XC.SEC.info-hiding`, and provides `C-INTEGRITY` (L3), which `prediction-rollback`, `dedicated-server`, `character-movement` and others consume.\n- Ruling rationale: the two reparentings are done. Keeping anti-cheat under security-engineering is technically sound. Much of its territory (offline score integrity, device attestation, user-mode posture) is not netcode. Its workstream stays `online`, and 00 §2 delegates arbitration by workstream, so its disputes with replication are settled inside the online workstream, not by the threat-model skill. Its runtime coupling to networking is carried by `C-INTEGRITY`.\n\n### K-COMPLETE-4 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `GAM.AI.team-visibility` (\"Per-faction visibility fields & line-of-sight/FOV (grid/hex/navmesh, GPU variant), explored state, deterministic, feeding fog rendering, AI knowledge and replication relevancy\"), owner `ai-behavior-perception` (profiles `all`), contributors `replication`, `anti-cheat-integrity`, `render-2d-vector`, `crowd-simulation`, maturity E. `crowd-simulation` has profiles `[\"openworld\",\"massim\"]`.\n- Ruling rationale: the omission (no owner for team visibility / FOV) is closed. The owner the builder chose is better than the proposed one: `crowd-simulation` does not exist in min2d roguelike configurations, while `ai-behavior-perception` exists in every profile.\n- Note: the contract path from this capability to replication relevancy and the fog overlay is not wired. This is ruled on under K-GAMEPLAY-12, where the critic asked for it explicitly.\n\n### K-FUTURE-1 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `RND.GRAPH.work-graphs` is X/`experimental`. The new `RND.RHI.work-graph-programs` is X/`experimental` (\"fallback: indirect dispatch\") and has its own radar entry, class X. `RND.GEO.mesh-nodes` also has an X radar entry. `RND.RHI.gpu-work` is E and now covers \"indirect, device-generated commands, backing memory\". `check.py` lines 452–459 enforce radar class = capability maturity, and its selftest mutates the work-graph entry to prove the rule. docs/00 §7 and docs/06 A6 agree.\n- Ruling rationale: the material problem was experimental work graphs presented as a production baseline. That is resolved. Keeping device-generated commands in E is sound: D3D12 ExecuteIndirect with state-changing command signatures and Metal indirect command buffers have shipped for years, and `VK_EXT_device_generated_commands` is a cross-vendor EXT. A separate M row would not change what phase-2 agents freeze.\n\n### K-FUTURE-7 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `ml-inference-runtime` has profiles `[\"all\"]`, parent `core-runtime-architect`, workstream `foundation`, and the new NR \"GPU queue/budget arbitration of C-MLGPU work → gpu-platform-architect\". `C-MLGPU` stays gated. `C-ML` (L2) requires only `C-TASK` and `C-MEM`.\n- Ruling rationale: every structural part of the proposal is applied. Because the profile is `all`, the runtime is in the `minimal-client` closure (75 build skills, closed by `check.py`), which is the proof the proposed `narrative-llm-client` configuration would have given. On-device LLM sequence execution (`ML.RT.sequence-models`) is X and so opt-in through `experimental`. That follows the maturity rules and is not a scale-down defect.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-2 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-ABILITY` L4 (gameplay-systems-toolkit: activation, effects, attributes, tag queries, messages, hit results). `C-CROWD` L4 (crowd-simulation: spawn/despawn, batched queries, LOD/tier hooks). `C-SEQ` L4 (cinematics-sequencer: playback, binding, event/track callbacks, skip/blend). Consumers: `ai-behavior-perception` → `C-ABILITY?`; `gameplay-architect` → `C-SEQ?`, `C-CROWD?`. `C-GAMEDATA` (L3) summary now includes \"gameplay tag registry and tag queries\", and `narrative-dialogue` consumes `C-GAMEDATA?`.\n- Ruling rationale: all three missing surfaces now exist and are consumed. Putting tags in the existing L3 `C-GAMEDATA` meets the critic's own constraint (\"tags must live at L3\"). A separate `C-TAGS` would add nothing. `C-SEQ` at L4 rather than L3 is harmless because its consumers are L4.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-AI` L4 (ai-behavior-perception: brain assignment for BT/state tree/utility/HTN, perception stimulus sources, smart-object claim/release, environment query service; learned/LLM providers plug in through `C-AIAGENT`). Consumers are `gameplay-architect`, `gameplay-systems-toolkit` and `crowd-simulation`. `ai-behavior-perception` consumes `C-MOVE?`. The `C-NAV` summary states \"nav produces path corridors and steering intents and never moves bodies\".\n- Ruling rationale: every item is present. A separate established contract with the gated extension point referenced from it is cleaner than renaming `C-AIAGENT`, because it keeps the gated/optional rule of 00 §7 intact.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-5 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: the `C-CFG` summary now says settings are declared as scoped C-CFG entries (device/user/cloud), and the user layer is a read-only projection populated by the persisted settings store (`GAM.SAVE.settings`). The `C-SAVE` summary adds \"persisted player-settings store per scope, populating the C-CFG user layer\". The `C-A11YRT` summary adds \"accessibility settings are C-CFG settings entries\". `GAM.SAVE.settings` is renamed to \"Persisted player-settings store per scope …, first-boot availability; settings declared through C-CFG\". `C-CFG` is `universal: runtime`, so input-system, accessibility and runtime-scalability can all register settings.\n- Ruling rationale: there is now one declaration path (C-CFG, available to every runtime skill including L1 `runtime-scalability`) and one persistence owner. That is the single-registry outcome the critic asked for, reached without the layering violation that `C-SAVE` consumption from L1 would cause.\n\n### K-GAMEPLAY-12 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `GAM.AI.team-visibility` exists (see K-COMPLETE-4). `GAM.SYS.markers` exists (gameplay-systems-toolkit, contributors ui-architect, render-architect). `UI.FW.maps` exists (ui-architect). `XC.SEC.info-hiding` exists (anti-cheat-integrity). Missing:\n  - `ai-behavior-perception` does not consume `C-REP`.\n  - The `C-AI` summary does not mention visibility queries.\n  - `anti-cheat-integrity` consumes neither `C-REP` nor `C-AI`. It provides L3 `C-INTEGRITY`, so it cannot consume L4 `C-AI`.\n  - `ui-architect` (L3 `C-UI`) cannot consume the toolkit's L4 contracts, and neither `gameplay-systems-toolkit` nor `C-ABILITY` exposes the marker registry.\n- Ruling rationale: the capabilities and owners are fine, including the owner changes. But the critic explicitly required \"C-REP relevancy hook consumption for the visibility item\". This is the anti-maphack path: the server withholds what a team cannot see. It is absent. As the data stands, the owner of team visibility has no contract through which to feed replication relevancy, fog rendering or the map service. Under 00 §3 its contributors could reach it only through internals. The material problem (a cross-domain system without a working seam) is therefore still half open.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add `C-REP?` to `ai-behavior-perception.consumes`. State in the `C-REP` summary, or in `GAM.AI.team-visibility`, that per-faction visibility registers as a relevancy filter through C-REP relevancy hooks. `XC.SEC.info-hiding` builds on that filter.\n  2. Add \"per-faction visibility / explored-state queries\" to the `C-AI` summary. State that the fog overlay receives the visibility field through a declared `C-FLOW` channel (ai-behavior-perception already consumes C-FLOW).\n  3. Give the marker registry a reachable surface for `UI.FW.maps`. For example, add the marker registry to the `C-ABILITY` (or `C-GAME`) summary and have `gameplay-systems-toolkit` consume `C-UI?` to publish markers into the L3 map service. Alternatively, put a marker-source interface in `C-UI` that the toolkit implements.\n\n### K-LEGACY-7 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `ecs-runtime.tool_consumes = [C-COOK]`, `world-architect.tool_consumes = [C-COOK]`, `render-architect.tool_consumes = [C-EDCMD, C-EDHOST]`, `geometry-pipeline.tool_consumes = [C-COOK]`. `check.py` lines 490–496 fail a runtime owner of a \"tool-side\" capability that has no tool contract. \"Tool-side\" is `TOOL_SIDE` (line 590): `cook step|authoring → runtime baking|bake pipeline|editor render features|generation orchestration`. 00 §3 (\"tool contracts only from tool_consumes\") keeps code that uses `C-COOK`/`C-EDHOST` out of client and server closures.\n- Ruling rationale: the four named capabilities are fixed, and deriving tool-side status from wording instead of a new field would be an acceptable mechanism. However, the regex is tailored to the literal names of the flagged capabilities, not to \"cook/bake/editor territory\" as the disposition claims. A cap such as \"texture baking\", \"cooked X\", \"editor support for Y\" or `XC.EXT.mod-sdk` (\"Mod SDK & tooling\") would not match. The guarantee the critic asked for (any cook/bake/editor capability's owner reaches a tool contract) is therefore not enforced for future edits, and the check's own claim overstates it. A broader wording rule passes against today's data. The only runtime-owned hit without tool contracts under `\\bcook|\\bbak(e|ed|ing)\\b|\\beditor\\b` is `ED.DEBUG.automation-protocol`, a legitimate runtime protocol.\n- Required change: broaden `TOOL_SIDE` in `scripts/check.py` to the general vocabulary, at minimum `\\bcook(ed|ing)?\\b|\\bbak(e|ed|ing)\\b|\\beditor\\b|\\bSDK\\b.*tool|modder editor|authoring tool`. Add an explicit exemption list (initially `ED.DEBUG.automation-protocol`, plus any other runtime protocol the builder justifies). Add a selftest mutation, for example emptying `ecs-runtime.tool_consumes` must go red via `CORE.ECS.baking`, so the guard is known to fire.\n\n### K-LEGACY-12 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `WLD.PART.grid`, `.sources` and `.hlod` carry `[\"lite3d\",\"std3d\"]`. `WLD.PART.server` carries `[\"openworld\"]`, and `WLD.PART.sim-tiers` carries `[\"openworld\",\"massim\"]`. The new `WLD.MODEL.unit-load` (\"Async non-blocking whole-unit load with loading-screen hand-off (minimal/2D default)\") is owned by world-architect, E. L18 stance: \"Async non-blocking loading; cell streaming with budgeted activation for 3D/openworld\", with stance caps `WLD.PART.activation` and `WLD.MODEL.unit-load`.\n- Ruling rationale: minimal and min2d closures no longer contain cell streaming or HLOD, and the new-dogma wording of L18 is fixed. Gating on lite3d/std3d instead of openworld is sound, because linear 3D titles also stream sub-levels.\n- Note (non-binding): `WLD.PART.cook` (\"World → streaming-cell cook step\") is still untagged. Give it the same `[\"lite3d\",\"std3d\"]` tags as `WLD.PART.grid` for consistency.\n\n### K-NET-8 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `NET.ARCH.validation` = \"Netcode oracle scenarios & acceptance thresholds (co-signed by simulation-validation per QA.AGENT.oracle-change-control)\", contributor `simulation-validation`. `QA.SIM.netsim` = \"Deterministic single-process network-simulation runner, metrics & tolerance store\". `QA.FUNC.network` = \"Multi-client bot harness & scripted network sessions\". `NET.TRANS.simulation` = \"Network link conditioner seam (latency, loss, jitter)\". `QA.AGENT.oracle-change-control` requires quality-skill co-signature on every `*.validation` change. The contract conformance suites have independent oracle authors (`C-NET` → online-performance, `C-REP` → prediction-rollback).\n- Ruling rationale: the four-way overlap now has explicit, disjoint boundaries. The self-grading risk is removed by a different mechanism: the network lead can no longer change scenarios or thresholds without the independent quality skill (workstream quality), and the runner and metrics belong to simulation-validation. This matches the physics, animation and audio pattern and is consistent with the render-validation precedent.\n\n### K-NET-19 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `NET.ARCH.meshing` is M, and its radar entry is class M (evidence: Star Citizen live since late 2024; fallback: zoning). docs/06 A5 says \"`emerging` (M) in data and radar\". New entries: `NET.TRANS.l4s` (X, radar, fallback standard CC), `NET.REP.moq-spectator` (X, radar, fallback delayed spectator replication), `XC.SEC.behavioral-detection` (M, radar, fallback rule-based validation), `XC.SEC.usermode-anticheat` (M, radar \"Windows Resiliency Initiative… Proton\", fallback kernel middleware where permitted).\n- Ruling rationale: the three label sources now agree, which was part (a). Every radar gap in part (b) has an owned, fallback-bearing entry. M for meshing is a justified reading of \"emerging production\" given a live commercial deployment.\n\n### K-PLATFORM-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-PAL.needs_implementer = true`. The summary says \"interfaces and policy owned by platform-architect; each platform's implementation is a registered backend implemented by that platform's skill\". `implements` contains `C-PAL` for platform-desktop, -console, -mobile, -web and -server-host. There are new per-platform capabilities `PLAT.DESK.pal`, `PLAT.CON.pal`, `PLAT.MOB.pal`, `PLAT.WEB.pal` and `PLAT.SRV.pal`. `check.py` enforces a per-platform implementer for every `needs_implementer` contract in each configuration. `ARCH.STRUCT.platform-backends` lists the slots.\n- Ruling rationale: who writes the Win32 window versus the PAL interface is now decided in data, and it is proven per configuration and platform. A separate `slots` field would duplicate the capability text.\n\n### K-PLATFORM-6 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `platform-console` and `rhi-console` have `access = \"nda:per-platform-holder\"` and `instances = \"one agent per platform-holder family\"`. `check.py` lines 236–239 require console-only skills to be NDA and NDA skills to be console-only. The new `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots` (platform-console) lists IO/decompression, audio endpoints, save storage, crash upload, system keyboard and entitlement SDK. The `C-ORCH` summary adds access classes and NDA routing. Still present: `RES.IO.backends` = \"Async IO backends (io_uring, IOCP, DirectStorage, **console APIs**)\", owned by public `async-io-storage`. `PLAT.XR.runtime-backends` = \"… **console XR SDK (confidential slot)**\", owned by public `xr-runtime` and not listed in `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots`.\n- Ruling rationale: the access class, the per-holder instantiation and the consolidated slot capability are acceptable alternative mechanisms. However, the critic's core issue was confidential slot implementations owned by skills with no access class, which program-orchestration cannot route. Two public skills still own confidential console territory, and one of them overlaps directly with `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots` (IO). The critic's proposed guard (\"no public skill owns a capability marked nda\") has no equivalent, which is why `check.py` does not catch either case.\n- Required change:\n  1. Reword `RES.IO.backends` to drop \"console APIs\", or say \"console variants via PLAT.CON.confidential-slots\".\n  2. Move the console XR SDK slot out of `PLAT.XR.runtime-backends` into `PLAT.CON.confidential-slots`, adding `xr-runtime` as a contributor.\n  3. Add a `check.py` rule, with a selftest mutation: a capability whose name matches `confidential|console (API|SDK)` (exempting the mechanism capability `PLAT.PAL.confidential-extensions` and public-interface wording such as \"behind public interfaces\") must be owned by a skill whose `access` starts with `nda`.\n\n### K-PLATFORM-12 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: targets are platform-mobile `[\"client\",\"tools\"]`, platform-console `[\"client\",\"tools\"]` and platform-web `[\"client\",\"tools\"]`. `C-TARGETPLAT` is an L5 `needs_implementer` contract (\"per-target-platform tool modules: cook variants, shader backend hook, packager, deploy/launch/debug\"), implemented by all platform skills and tool-consumed by content-pipeline-architect and packaging-release-patching.\n- Ruling rationale: the spurious headless-client/server targets are removed. The tools target now has real content (target-platform tool modules loaded by the PC editor and cooker) and does not imply web- or console-hosted tools.\n\n### K-PROD-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `reference-games` has kind `runtime`, targets client/headless-client/server/tools, and consumes `C-GAME, C-INPUT?, C-UI?, C-SCRIPT?, C-GAMEDATA?, C-API`. It owns new `QA.REF.game-code` (public-API only), `QA.REF.content-acquisition` (licensed/purchased/scanned/procedural/generated with rights manifest; contributors content-pipeline-architect, which owns `CNT.ID.rights`, and procedural-generation) and `QA.REF.upkeep` (migration each release, feeding `ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus`). It is in milestone M1, and `QA.REF.ladder` is a gate.\n- Ruling rationale: the three unowned responsibilities now have an owner that is a real build skill consuming the public API, which is the dogfooding the critic wanted. Whether that owner is staffed as one agent or a team is a staffing matter that `ARCH.ORG.staffing` governs, not a boundary defect.\n- Note (non-binding): `ai-assisted-authoring` (proposed contributor) is not on `QA.REF.content-acquisition`. It reaches the rights chain through `CNT.ID.rights`/`ED.AI.provenance`, so this is not material.\n\n### K-PROD-7 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: the milestones run M0–M7. M1 is \"Indie 2D slice on PC\" (runtime only). M2 is \"ships everywhere, with tools\" and includes a TRC/store-review dry run (gate `QA.CERT.prechecks`), with editor contracts drafted at M2. M4 adds \"patch + DLC + staged rollout + rollback executed on the online reference game\" (gates `BLD.REL.staged-rollout`, `BLD.REL.rollback`). M7 is \"Engine 1.0 / first LTS\": corpus upgrade, release notes, backport stream, console cert pass, live-ops and end-of-service rehearsal (gates `ARCH.PROD.customer-corpus`, `XC.EXT.lts`, `BLD.REL.end-of-service`, `PLAT.LIVE.operations`). I computed the M1 closure: 39 of the 44 M1 skills are required by `indie-2d-client@pc ∪ minimal-client@pc` beyond M0. The other 5 (network-architect, network-transport, replication, prediction-rollback, net-session) are placed there deliberately as contract-drafting design authorities, per the M1 exit text.\n- Ruling rationale: every missing ship-lifecycle milestone the critic listed is present, and the M1a/M1b split is in effect: editor/tools and non-PC platforms move to M2. The remaining M1 size is forced by the minimal-client closure, not by bundling, except for five network authorities placed early on purpose.\n- Notes (non-binding): (1) the disposition's statement that the configuration \"requires\" all 44 skills is accurate for 39. (2) The critic's last bullet, milestone exit as test-architect evidence plus a human gate, is only partly covered: the gates are capabilities with owners, and the program-orchestration vs reference-games independence pair exists. `ARCH.ORG.human-gates` does not list milestone exit sign-off. Adding it is recommended.\n\n### K-SEC-14 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `BLD.SYS.dev-surface-exclusion` (\"Dev-surface manifest per skill, fitness function and binary scan proving shipping configurations contain none; allowed shipping surfaces authenticated via C-IPC\"), owned by build-system-toolchains with contributors security-engineering and architecture-governance. `dev_surfaces` is declared on platform-architect (devlink), observability-telemetry (remote diagnostics), visual-debugging-tools (console & cheats, automation endpoint), hot-reload-iteration (live coding, reload endpoint) and scripting-runtime (script debugger (DAP)). `ED.ARCH.remote` belongs to `editor-architect`, which is tool-only, so its on-device endpoint is the devlink/automation/reload surfaces already declared. The M1 gate includes `BLD.SYS.dev-surface-exclusion`.\n- Ruling rationale: this is what the critic proposed, and a fitness function plus binary scan as the capability's deliverable, gated at M1, is the mechanism the critic asked for. The critic did not ask for a check.py rule.\n- Note (non-binding): the `skills.json` `_doc` does not document the `dev_surfaces` field. The `C-IPC` summary still says only \"dev-only port security\" and could name the authenticated shipping-surface rule.\n\n### K-SIM-1 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `CORE.FRAME.pipelining` = \"Frame pipelining & frames in flight (incl. whether any dedicated render/RHI thread exists, by ADR)\". L03's stance is \"Render work as tasks in overlapping task-graph frames; dedicated threads only by ADR\", with `stance_capabilities` `CORE.FRAME.pipelining`, `RND.GPU.recording` and `CORE.JOBS.thread-model`. `check.py` lines 473–488 require every legacy pattern to name existing stance capabilities that its justification owner owns, contributes to or leads.\n- Ruling rationale: the capability no longer prescribes the legacy two-thread model and is tied to the L03 stance. Matching capability names against prose detection strings would be brittle. Linking every legacy pattern to the capabilities that carry its modern stance is a sound substitute, and it would have caught this case because the stance capability's wording is now reviewed against L03.\n\n### K-SIM-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `fluid-simulation.profiles = [\"aaa\",\"sandbox\",\"massim\"]`. `PHY.FLUID.cellular` (\"Grid/cellular material & fluid simulation (falling sand, liquid/gas/heat grids), 2D-capable, deterministic\"), E, contributor `physics-2d`. The configuration `sandbox-2d-client` (`min2d + sandbox`, pc) contains `fluid-simulation` and `PHY.FLUID.cellular` and is closed by `check.py`.\n- Ruling rationale: the critic's proposal explicitly allowed gating on sandbox/massim in addition to aaa. The missing Noita/ONI-class capability exists and a 2D configuration proves it.\n\n### K-SYSTEMS-3 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: `C-FRAME` is consumed by audio-architect, spatial-transforms, scripting-runtime, navigation-pathfinding, ai-behavior-perception, ui-architect, ml-inference-runtime and prediction-rollback. `C-FLOW` is consumed by audio-architect, physics-architect, animation-architect, replication, ai-behavior-perception, input-system and render-architect.\n- Ruling rationale: every edge the critic proposed exists. The proposed rule turns on \"schedules per-frame work\", which is not decidable from current data. Rejecting it is reasonable, and the concrete undeclared dependencies that caused the problem are gone.\n\n### K-TEST-2 · UPHOLD\n- Verified in data: the `QA.SIM` capabilities are renamed to artifacts: `stability-suite` (\"runner, metrics (penetration, energy drift) & tolerance store\"), `golden-traces` (\"comparison harness & baseline store\"), `netsim` (\"runner, metrics & tolerance store\"), `animation` (\"error metrics … & tolerance store\") and `audio` (\"metric library (BS.1770 loudness, glitch detection) & offline-render comparison harness\"). The domain `*.ARCH.validation` caps keep the scenario suites under `QA.AGENT.oracle-change-control`. `simulation-validation.consumes = [C-PHYS, C-ANIM, C-AUDIO, C-NET?, C-REPLAY]`. Its NR \"Domain scenario definitions\" now points to an explicit list (physics-, animation-, audio-, network-architect), and the `owning-skill` target is gone. The net four-way boundary is explicit (see K-NET-8).\n- Ruling rationale: every part of the proposal is applied. The retained split (scenarios in the domain, co-signed) is what the critic proposed (\"Keep scene and scenario definitions in the *.ARCH.validation caps\").\n\n### K-TOOLS-8 · OVERTURN\n- Verified in data: `C-EDIT` L4 (modding-ugc): serializable commands with the schema shared with C-EDCMD per `ED.ARCH.transactions` (which lists modding-ugc as contributor), undo, selection, permissions, budgets, publish/version. It is consumed by `gameplay-systems-toolkit` (`C-EDIT?`). New `XC.EXT.player-creation`, `XC.EXT.ugc-discovery` and `XC.EXT.mod-editor` (licence/NDA stripping, sandboxed tool plugins; contributors packaging-release-patching, platform-architect, security-engineering). `modding-ugc.tool_consumes = [C-EDCMD, C-EDHOST, C-COOK]`. Missing: `C-GRAPH` is still L5 tool-only (\"Graph authoring & compilation\"). `RND.SHADER.untrusted` covers only \"GPU-DoS limits for UGC-authored graphs/shaders\", and no capability or contract provides a runtime (client-target) graph compile path. `TOOL_SIDE` does not match `XC.EXT.mod-sdk` (\"Mod SDK & tooling\") or `XC.EXT.mod-editor`.\n- Ruling rationale: runtime edit sessions, the redistributable modder editor and modding-ugc's tool contracts are resolved. Sharing the command schema through `ED.ARCH.transactions` is a sound way to avoid an editor→UGC dependency. Two parts of the finding are still open:\n  - The critic named the missing \"runtime graph-compile path\" for in-client graph authoring (Dreams-class, and the UGC shaders `RND.SHADER.untrusted` anticipates). Nothing owns it, and the only compile surface (`C-GRAPH`) cannot ship in client builds.\n  - The disposition says the `mod-sdk` name rule is \"replaced by the general tool-side rule\", but that rule's regex does not match either mod-SDK capability, so the replacement does not exist in `check.py`.\n- Required change:\n  1. Add a capability for the client-side graph path, for example `RND.SHADER.ugc-runtime-compile` / `XC.EXT.runtime-graphs`: \"Runtime compilation of UGC-authored graphs (restricted node set, validation, shader/VM compile in client targets under RND.SHADER.untrusted limits)\", owner `shader-system` or `modding-ugc`, profile `ugc`, contributor `graph-editor-framework`. Expose it through `C-EDIT` (or a new L3/L4 runtime graph contract), and state that `C-GRAPH`'s node/pin model is the shared schema, as is done for commands.\n  2. Make the tool-side rule cover `XC.EXT.mod-sdk` and `XC.EXT.mod-editor`. This is satisfied by the broadened `TOOL_SIDE` required under K-LEGACY-7 if it includes `\\bSDK\\b.*tool|modder editor`.\n\n---\n\n**Summary: 24 findings adjudicated — 20 UPHOLD, 4 OVERTURN (K-GAMEPLAY-12, K-LEGACY-7, K-PLATFORM-6, K-TOOLS-8).**\n"}},"apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CfY61KHfW1vovXcsbR84p","attributionAgent":"general-purpose","type":"assistant","uuid":"bc92302f-eb75-4408-8226-8d4eca7ad55b","timestamp":"2026-09-29T17:01:27.456Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","userType":"external","entrypoint":"remote_mobile","cwd":"/home/user/skills","sessionId":"64f15158-6a4e-5bdf-8a90-7f2bd7b05b82","version":"2.1.284","gitBranch":"claude/engine-skill-framework-phase1","slug":"warm-pondering-kernighan"}
